The genealogy of Jesus.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah was the father of Perez and Zerah. Their mother was Tamar. Perez was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salem was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of King David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Ahaz.
Azariah was the father of Utham. Utham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jonathan and his brothers. And in that time, the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Jonadab was the father of Shillem. Shillem was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azor was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Akim. Akim was the father of Eliud.
Elimelech was the father of Noemi. Noemi was the mother of Ismaele. Ismaele was the father of Seguel.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
So there were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 from David until the Jewish people had to leave for Babylon. And there were 14 from that time to Christ. The birth of Jesus Christ
Thus Jesus Christ was born. The mother of Jesus, Mary, promised John that he would marry her. But before they began living together, it became clear that she was pregnant. She conceived powerfully by the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a pious man. He did not want to shame her publicly. So he decided to divorce her quietly.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as wife, for the child in her womb is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You must give him the name Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All of this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The virgin girl will give birth to a son. She will bring forth a male child. And his name will be called Immanuel (which means "God with us").
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not consummate their marriage until she had given birth to a son. And Joseph called the child Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This took place while Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and everyone in Jerusalem was also disturbed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the teachers of the law. He asked them where the Messiah would be born.
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they replied. "For this is what the prophet has written: 'The Lord was pleased to come from his dwelling place in heaven to live on earth. He chose a town in Judea, the small and insignificant town of Bethlehem. From this town he chose to come, the one who is to be our ruler, the one foretold by the prophets of old.'"
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will rule over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. When he had learned the exact time from them, he sent them
Send them to Bethlehem. And he said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. And when you have found him, bring me word. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. And the star which they had seen when they were in the east went before them. And when they had come to the house, they saw the young child with Mary his mother, and fell down, and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures, and presented him with gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The sage went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. The flight of the Holy Family into Egypt
When the wise man left, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, the angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Get up!" the angel said. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to search for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph awoke. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
And they remained there until Herod the king died. Then an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, "Get up, take your son, and go to the land of
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He gave orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All young boys under the age of two were to be killed. This corresponded to the time that the wise men had seen the star.
And in this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said,
A voice was heard in Ramah. It was the voice of weeping and great sorrow. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone." (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Arise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Then Joseph arose, and took the Child and His mother Mary, and returned with them to the land of Egypt.
But then he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After being warned in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a town called Nazareth. So what the prophets had said about Jesus was true. They had said, 'He will be called a Nazarene.'
John the Baptist paves the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent of your sins, for the Kingdom of Heaven is closer than you think."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and all Judah, and also from all the region around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized his followers in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. And he said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath? "
Bear fruit that shows you have turned away from your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The sickle is already lying at the roots of the trees. All trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for you to repent of your sins, and I baptize you with the Holy Spirit, and with fire.
He carries his censer in his hand to remove the straw from the place of the harvest. He will gather the grain of the wheat into the storage rooms. But he will burn the husk with the fire that is never quenched. Jesus is baptized.
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. "I need to baptize *you*," John told Jesus. "So why are *you* coming to *me*?"
Jesus answered: "Leave her alone, just as she is. It is proper and right for us to do this. He is fulfilling the holy plan of God." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. At that moment the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God come down on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Spirit of God had led Jesus into the wilderness. There the devil had made him undergo a temptation.
After 40 days and 40 nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The wicked one came to him. He said, "If you are the Son of God, command this stone to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. And he stopped him at the highest place of the mosque.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' (From Psalm 91:11-12)
Jesus answered, saying, "It is written: 'You shall not test the Lord your God'" (Deuteronomy 6:16).
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you prostrate yourself and obey me," he said, "then I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only' " (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil departed from Jesus. And behold, angels came and ministered to him. Jesus begins to be glorified.
John was put in prison, and when Jesus heard that, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And in this way, the words of the prophet Isaiah were fulfilled. For he said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who are now living in the darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But the light will shine on them. (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking along the shores of the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were fishermen, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew, they were casting their net into the lake.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
And from there, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And as they were preparing the two fishing nets, Jesus called out to them.
So they left the ship and their father in his condition, and they followed Jesus. Jesus heals sick people.
Jesus traveled throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the gospel of the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and every weakness that was in the people.
News about him spread all over Syria. People brought everyone who was sick with all kinds of diseases. Some were suffering from terrible pain. Others were controlled by demons. Some were shaking violently. Others couldn't move at all. Jesus healed all these people.
A great multitude followed him. Some people came from Galilee, and from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. And others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowd. He went up into a mountain, and when he had sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
Blessed are the needy, for to them belongs the Kingdom of the Heavens.
Blessed are those, who, remaining awake, are vigilant for Him. For them is their reward with their Lord; nor shall they there experience fear, nor shall they grieve.
Blessed are the meek. They shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those with soft and sensitive hearts, for they shall be truly satisfied.
Blessed are the merciful, for the merciful will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the people of the pure hearts. They will see God.
Blessed are the people of reconciliation. They are in God's mercy.
Blessed are those who suffer, for the sake of doing good works. For theirs is the Kingdom of the heavens.
Blessed are you when people revile you and persecute you on my account.
Rejoice and be glad. Your reward in heaven is great. Likewise, people hurt prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, what good is it? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.
People don't light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then the light shines for everyone in the house.
"Similarly, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good deeds and give glory to your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law"
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them.
What I am going to tell you is a fact. The heavens and the earth will pass away before the letters of the law disappear. Not even at the time of the end will any trade of the crafts of the pen disappear.
Do not break any of the commandments, even the least important one. And do not teach others to break them. If you do this, you will be called the least important people in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice these commandments and teach them. Then you will be called important people in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
I have heard what was said to the people who lived a long time ago. It was said to them: "You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) Whoever murders will be put to death."
But here's what I'm telling you. Don't be angry with your brother. Everyone who is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. Again, everyone who says to his brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' is in danger of the council. But whoever says, 'You fool!' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Imagine you are presenting your gift at the altar. And remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Imagine someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on your way. If you do not do that, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. You are not leaving until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But I tell you, do not lust after a woman, not even in your heart. Whoever looks at a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. For your eye is a part of your body. If you then make your eye into a means of committing adultery, it would be better to lose that eye than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
Indeed, if something from the right afflicts you, and you want to be tested by it, then cut it off, and cut off the root of the right. Indeed, the right is something, and the Hour is something. And indeed, a man will be given the trial in his hand, and what he possesses, and he will make with it what he will, then it will be against him as a burden.
It was said: "If a man divorces his wife while she is alive, he shall write her a certificate of divorce in accordance with the law." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But here is what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And anyone who marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man cannot divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. The oath.
And again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told: "Do not break the covenant you have made with the Lord your God. Keep the commandments of the Lord your God, and His blessing will rest on your land, on your grain, and on your wine, and on everything you put your hand to."
But what I am telling you is this: do not say anything of the sort. Do not say anything of the sort, for heaven's sake. It is God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the earth. That is the footstool of God. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the Great King.
Do not swear by your heads, for you cannot make a single white or black hair.
Let "yes" mean "yes." Let "no" mean "no." Anything more than this comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth" (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here's what I tell you. Don't fight an evil person. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek. Offer him your left cheek as well.
If someone takes you to court to get your shirt, let him have your coat also.
Assume someone forces you to walk one mile. Go with him two miles.
Give to the one who asks of you. Do not hate the one who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor." (Deuteronomy 19:18) "Hate your enemies."
But here is what I tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
Then you are the children of the patriarch who is in heaven. "He lets out the rain from heaven and makes for you rivers and springs of water and gives you grain and wine and oil, and whatever your heart desires, in order that you may live in plenty. The L ord your God is blessed.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you receive? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet people of your own nation, what more are you doing than others? Even people who don't believe do that.
So do not be of differing opinions concerning what comes to you of the Message
Donating to the poor.
Beware of doing "good works" before others. Do not do them so that others may see. If you do so, your father in heaven will not reward you.
 "When you give to needy people, do not announce it by having trumpets blown. Do not be like those who only pretend to be holy. They announce what they do in the synagogues and on the streets. They want to be honored by others. What I'm about to tell you is true. They have received their complete reward.   
When you give to the poor, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then, indeed, your charity will be kept secret. Your Father rewards you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer
"When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing so that they may be seen by others. Truly, I tell you, they have received their reward in full. But you, when you pray, go into your room, and when you have shut your door, pray to your Father who is in secret, and your Father who sees in secret will reward you. And when you are praying, do not use meaningless repetition as the Gentiles do, for they suppose that they will be heard because of their many words. Therefor do not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask him. Pray then like this: Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name. Thy kingdom come, thy will be done, on earth as it is in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread, and forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors. And lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil. For thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen."
"When you arrive, enter your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your reward will be given to you. Your Father sees what you hide."
When you arrive, do not speak much like the wicked people do. They think they will be listened to because they speak a lot.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
Thus you should pray: "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
We have come to Your Kingdom. Let Your will be done on Earth as it is in Heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our sins, as we have forgiven those who have wronged us.
Protect us from falling into sin when we are tempted. Protect us from the accursed Satan.
Forgive people, Muhammad, when they sin. If you do that, your father who is in heaven will forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
 "When you go without eating, do not look gloomy like those who only pretend to be holy. They make their faces very sad. They want to show people they are fasting. What I'm about to tell you is true. They have received their complete reward.   
But if they are silent, anoint your heads with olive oil. Wash your faces.
Then no one will know that you are fasting, except your father - who is in the heavens. Indeed, your father - he is certainly watchful. So as for whoever intended to do you harm - then he will only have [a means of] conveying information. And as for whoever intended to do you evil, then he will have suffering. And God is powerful and wise. Spend from what We have provided you before a face is turned to face - and you are, then, overcome by grief - and you will have no way of resolving anything. At that, you will see the criminals in what they have committed in despair.
Do not make the earth's treasury a possession for yourselves. Lest the moth consume it, and the most vile spoil it. And the thieves enter into it and steal it.
Rather, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Imagine that your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are weak. Then your whole body will be dark. And if the light within you is darkness, then that means it is very dark!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry
I tell you, don't worry. Don't worry about your life, what you will eat or drink. And don't worry about your body, what you will wear. For isn't life more than food, and the body more than clothing?
Look at the birds of the air: for they neither sow nor reap, nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much better than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why are you worried about clothes? Look at how the wild flowers grow. They don't work or make clothes.
But here is what I tell you. Not even Solomon in all his glory was arrayed like those lilies.
If God clothes the wild grasses this way, will he not clothe you even better? For the wild grasses are here today only, and tomorrow are thrown into the fire. What little faith this is!
Therefore do not worry, neither say, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
People who don't believe are chasing all those things. Your heavenly father knows you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do whatever he tells you to do. Then all these things will be given to you as well.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will have worries of its own. Each day has its own troubles.
Be fair when judging others.
Do not judge people, and you will not be judged.
You will be held accountable in the same way that you hold others accountable. You will be measured in the same way that you measure others.
You are looking at a splinter of wood in your friend's eye. But you do not care about the speck in your own eye.
How do you say to your friend: "Let me take a piece of wood out of your eye"? How do you say this while a piece of wood is in your eye?
Hypocrite! First, take the plank out of your own eye; then you will be able to see clearly to take the splinter out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to dogs, and do not throw your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet and turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and you shall receive, seek and you shall find, knock and it shall be opened to you.
To him who asks shall be given. To him who knocks shall be opened. Seek and you will find.
Imagine if your son asked for bread. Which one of you would give him a stone?
Or let's say he asked for a fish. Which of you would give him a snake?
Even if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give to those who ask him!
In everything, do good to others as you would have them do to you. This is in the Quran and in the early and later messengers. The great door and the small door.
Enter God's kingdom through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is wide that leads to destruction and hell. Many people take this road.
But the gate is small and the way is narrow that leads to life. Few people find the way. A tree and its fruits.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can tell what they really are by what they do. "Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?"
On the same note, every good tree bears good fruit, but the wicked tree bears wicked fruit.
A good tree does not bear bad fruit, and a bad tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then I clearly tell them: "I have never known you. Depart from me, you evildoers." The wise builder and the fools.
Every one who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The sky is raining. The waters are rising. The winds are blowing and hitting that house. But it is not falling down. It was built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not apply them is like a foolish man. He builds his house on the sand.
The sky is raining. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. And it falls with a big noise.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain. A great crowd followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can make me 'clean'."
Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man, and said, "I want to do this. Be 'clean'!" The man was immediately healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not say this to anyone. Go, show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. For this will be a testimony to them." The Faith of a Roman Centurion
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
"Lord," he said, "my servant is at home and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy to have you enter my house. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed.
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, "Go," and he goes. I say to this one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. And he said to those who were following him, "Truly I tell you, I have not found anyone in Israel with such great faith.
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west, and will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. And they will be seated with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.
But those who think they are from my kingdom will be cast out into the darkness. And there they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman soldiers, "Go! It shall be as you have believed." And his servants were healed at that hour. Jesus heals many.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's wife. She was lying down on the bed. She had a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. He cast out the spirits with a word. And he healed all who were ill.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He has borne our illnesses and carried our sorrows (Isaiah 53:4)." Following Jesus has a price.
Jesus saw the crowd around him. He gave his disciples orders to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of religious law came to him. He said, "O teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The dead have tombs, and the birds of the sky have nests. But as for the Son of Man, nowhere is there a place for him to lay his head."
Another disciple said to him, "Lord, first permit me to go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm.
Jesus entered a boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a terrible storm came upon the lake. The waves tossed the boat about. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples then came and woke him up. And they said, "Master, carest thou not that we perish? We are in great distress and danger of sinking!"
He answered, "O you of little faith! Why do you hesitate?" Then Jesus arose and rebuked the winds and the waves, and a great calm came upon the sea, so that it became like a great lake, calm and tranquil.
The disciples were amazed. And they asked, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men who were controlled by demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of Decapolis. Two men who were possessed by demons met him. They had come from the tombs. The men were so violent that no one could travel on that road.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried out. "Have you come here to punish us before the time when we are to be judged?"
Not far away, there was a large herd of pigs eating.
The demons requested of Jesus: "If you cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the unclean spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The herd of pigs all rushed down the steep bank into the lake. And they drowned in the water.
