They were asking him to allow the sick to touch the edge of his robe. And he healed all those who touched him.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why don't your students obey what the elders teach them? Your students don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the command of God? But you prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). And He also said, " 'Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death.' " (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you permit people to say to their parents: "Any help they received from us is a separate gift to God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God void for the sake of your traditions.
"O hypocrites! Isaiah was indeed righteous when he prophesied about you. He said:
"These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me."
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only man-made rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. He said, "Listen and understand."
What enters into the mouths of you all from the food does not defile you, but what comes out from the mouths of you all, that defiles you.
Then the disciples came to him. And they said, "Do you not understand that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my heavenly Father has not planted; these will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Have you not yet understood?" Jesus asked them.
What do you feel? Everything that enters the mouth enters the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. Also theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
This is what makes you "unclean". But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean". The faith of a Canaanite woman.
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon has seized my daughter. She suffers greatly."
Jesus did not say a word. Therefore, his disciples came to him. And they asked him: "Send her away. She is screaming behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the bread of children and throw it to their dog."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Then Jesus said: "Woman, you have great faith! It will be done for you as you ask." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus feeds four thousand people.
Jesus left there. He made his way along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to a mountain hill and sat down.
Great crowds came to him. They brought the blind and those who could not walk. They also brought people who were crippled, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. The blind could see. And the people thanked the God of Israel.
Then Christ directed His words towards His disciples and said: " 'These people have been a burden to me, for they have stayed with me for three days and have had no food. I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint along the way.' "
His disciples answered him. They said, "Nothing is here. Where then will we find enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
"How much bread do you have?" Jesus asked. They answered, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the crowds, "Sit on the ground."
He took the seven loaves and the fish, and gave thanks to God. Then he broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples picked up seven baskets of leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowd away, he went up into the boat. Then he went into the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He replied, "In the evening, you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "Today there will be a storm. The sky is red and overcast." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
Indeed, a bad and treacherous people are seeking a miraculous sign. But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." Then Jesus left them and departed. The experts concluded.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves about this. And they said: "He must be saying this because we did not take bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said to them, "Why do you worry, saying, 'We lack the things we need'?"
Don't you understand? What about the five loaves for the 5000? What about the number of baskets you collected?
Don't you remember the seven loaves for the 4000? Don't you remember how many baskets of leftovers you collected?
How is it that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus had not told them to beware of the yeast of bread. Rather, He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. And he asked his disciples there, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say it is John the Baptist; others say it is Elijah; and still others say it is Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Mr. James son of Zebedee! This one will teach you nothing unless someone from heaven instructs him."
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then He revealed to His disciples, "Do not report about Him to anyone, for He is the Messiah of God." Pay attention, for in this He is being tested. Pay attention, for in the Hereafter He will be guided.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what was going to happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer many things at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and on the third day he must rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. For you are not thinking about the things of God, but about the things of people."
Then Jesus spoke with his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What profit is there in gaining the whole world and losing your own soul? Or what can a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father, and with Him will come His angels. And He will reward each one according to his works.
I am not able to tell you, except the truth. Truly, some of those who stand here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom."
Change Jesus' appearance.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, and led them up a high mountain. They were all alone.
There before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, if you are to restore the kingdom to Israel now, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three dwellings for you—one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter was still speaking, a dark cloud came upon them, and a voice came from within the cloud, saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were filled with fear and fell face down to the ground.
But Jesus came to touch them. And He said to them, "Arise. Do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And on the way down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Wait until the Son of Man is raised to life."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is so written." It is supposed that Elijah will appear again and make all things new.
But I tell you, Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted. Likewise, they will also make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus feeds a boy from whom a demon had been cast out.
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus; he knelt down before him.
"Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is shaking violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples. But they were not able to heal him.
"O ye of little faith! How long shall I bear with you?"
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came, and they asked Jesus privately, "Why could we not cast it out?"
He answered, "Because you have so little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." So the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus walks up to the temple
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does not your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. And when Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, my children?" He asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth collect taxes and tribute? Do they collect it from their own sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children ought not to pay," Jesus said to him.
But we do not want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your fishing hook. Take the very first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find exactly the coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tribute for me and your brothers.
